2. Introduction

Software systems require continuous maintenance and improvement to remain useful, reliable, and efficient. As technology develops and user requirements change, existing software applications may become outdated, slow, or difficult to maintain.

Software maintenance is the process of modifying and updating software after its initial development to correct errors, improve performance, and adapt to changing requirements.

Software reengineering involves examining an existing software system and modifying or restructuring it to improve its quality, maintainability, and functionality without completely developing a new system from scratch.

Artificial Intelligence (AI) can also support software engineering by helping developers identify errors, generate code suggestions, analyze software performance, and improve testing activities.

In the given case study, a college uses an old Student Management System that has become slow, contains outdated features, and frequently produces errors. Software maintenance, reengineering, and AI-assisted techniques can be applied to modernize the system and improve its overall performance.

3. Problem Analysis

The existing Student Management System faces several problems that affect its performance and usability.

3.1 Slow System Performance

The system takes a long time to load student records, process requests, and generate reports. This reduces productivity and creates inconvenience for students and administrative staff.

3.2 Outdated Features

The existing application may not support modern requirements such as online fee payments, automated attendance tracking, real-time notifications, and online access to academic records.

3.3 Frequent Software Errors

The system frequently produces errors during operations such as student registration, updating records, and generating examination results. These errors can affect data accuracy and reliability.

3.4 Difficult Maintenance

The existing software may contain poorly organized code, outdated technologies, and insufficient documentation. This makes it difficult for developers to identify problems and introduce new features.

3.5 Limited Accessibility

The older system may only be accessible through specific computers or local networks, making it difficult for students and faculty members to access information from different locations.

Therefore, the college needs to improve the existing system through suitable maintenance activities, software reengineering, and AI-assisted development techniques.

4. Types of Software Maintenance

Software maintenance can be classified into four major types.

4.1 Corrective Maintenance

Corrective maintenance involves identifying and fixing errors or defects discovered after software deployment.

Application in the case study:

The college can use corrective maintenance to fix errors in student registration, attendance calculations, fee processing, and examination result generation.

4.2 Adaptive Maintenance

Adaptive maintenance involves modifying software to ensure that it continues to work with changes in its operating environment.

Application in the case study:

The Student Management System can be updated to support newer operating systems, database versions, web browsers, and modern hardware.

4.3 Perfective Maintenance

Perfective maintenance focuses on improving existing software functionality, performance, and usability according to user requirements.

Application in the case study:

The college can improve system speed, introduce a better user interface, add online fee payment facilities, and provide improved student performance reports.

4.4 Preventive Maintenance

Preventive maintenance involves making changes to software to reduce the possibility of future problems.

Application in the case study:

Developers can improve code quality, remove unnecessary code, update documentation, and regularly review the database and software components to prevent future failures.

5. Software Reengineering

Software reengineering is the process of analyzing, modifying, and restructuring an existing software system to improve its quality and maintainability.

Instead of replacing the entire Student Management System, the college can modernize its existing application by improving its architecture, code, database, and functionality.

5.1 Steps Involved in Software Reengineering

Step 1: Inventory Analysis

The existing software is examined to identify its components, features, technical problems, and areas that require improvement.

For example, the college can identify outdated modules related to attendance, student registration, and fee management.

Step 2: Documentation Analysis

Existing software documentation is reviewed to understand the system's architecture, functionality, database structure, and operational procedures.

Missing or outdated documentation is updated.

Step 3: Reverse Engineering

Reverse engineering involves analyzing the existing software to understand its internal structure, design, and functionality.

This helps developers understand how different modules interact with each other.

Step 4: Code Restructuring

The existing source code is reorganized to improve readability, remove unnecessary code, reduce duplication, and make future modifications easier.

Step 5: Data Restructuring

The database is examined and improved to remove inconsistencies, reduce duplication, and maintain accurate student information.

Step 6: Forward Engineering

The improved system is developed by implementing updated designs, improved code, modern technologies, and additional features.

Step 7: Testing and Deployment

The reengineered system is tested to ensure that all major functions work correctly. After successful testing, the modernized application is deployed for students, faculty, and administrative staff.

6. Applications of AI in Software Engineering

Artificial Intelligence can support developers in improving software quality, identifying errors, and automating repetitive development activities.

The following are two important applications of AI in software engineering.

6.1 AI-Assisted Code Generation and Code Review

AI-powered development tools can analyze existing source code, suggest improvements, identify potential errors, and generate code based on developer instructions.

Application in the case study:

Developers can use AI-assisted tools to examine the old Student Management System, identify inefficient code, suggest improvements, and assist in developing new features.

For example, AI tools can help developers generate code for an online student registration module or suggest ways to improve existing attendance management functionality.

AI-generated code must still be reviewed and tested by developers before implementation.

6.2 AI-Based Software Testing and Bug Detection

AI can assist software testing by analyzing application behavior, identifying possible defects, generating test cases, and detecting unusual patterns.

Application in the case study:

AI-assisted testing tools can help identify errors in student registration, fee calculations, attendance records, and examination result processing.

They can also help developers discover potential problems before the updated system is released.

This reduces manual testing effort and helps improve software reliability.

7. Expected Improvements After Modernization

The combination of software maintenance, reengineering, and AI-assisted techniques can provide several improvements.

7.1 Improved System Performance

Optimizing existing code, improving database operations, and removing unnecessary processes can reduce loading times and make the application faster.

7.2 Increased Reliability and Accuracy

Corrective maintenance and AI-assisted testing can help identify and resolve errors, improving the accuracy of student records, attendance information, and examination results.

7.3 Better Functionality and User Experience

The modernized system can introduce updated features such as online fee payments, improved attendance tracking, student portals, and automated notifications.

This makes the application more convenient for students, faculty members, and administrative staff.

